  {
    "knowledge_point": {
      "db_id": 17592186133895,
      "id": "5f4f726e-d985-5915-a4d0-30cfa6f4b89b",
      "title": "Identifying Vector Fields That Satisfy the Unit Curl Property"
    },
    "question": {
      ":question/problem": "Let $C$ be a simple, piecewise-smooth, closed, and positively oriented curve. Given that the line integral\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r\n$$\n\n \nrepresents the area of the region $D$ enclosed by any such curve in ${R}^{2},$ which of the following could be the vector field $F(x,y)?$\n\nI. $F(x,y)=⟨\\frac{y}{3} + e^{x + 3 y},\\,\\frac{4 x}{3} + 3 e^{x + 3 y}⟩$\n\nII. $F(x,y)=⟨2 y e^{x},\\,x - 2 e^{x}⟩$\n\nIII. $F(x,y)=⟨2 y^{3} - \\frac{y}{3},\\,6 x y^{2} + \\frac{2 x}{3}⟩$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "According to Green's theorem, if $F(x,y)=⟨P(x,y),\\,Q(x,y)⟩,$ where\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=1,\n$$\n\nwe can write the area of the region $D$ enclosed by the curve $C$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Area}(D) & ={∬}_{D}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}x+Q\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each pair in turn.\n\n- Vector field I gives the required area. In this case,\n$\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=3 e^{x + 3 y} + \\frac{1}{3}$\nand\n$\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=3 e^{x + 3 y} + \\frac{4}{3},$\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\left(3 e^{x + 3 y} + \\frac{4}{3}\\right)-\\left(3 e^{x + 3 y} + \\frac{1}{3}\\right)=\\left(\\frac{4}{3}\\right)+\\left(3 e^{x + 3 y}\\right)-\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)-\\left(3 e^{x + 3 y}\\right)=1.\n$$\n\n- Vector field II does *not* give the required area. Notice that\n$\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=2 e^{x}$\nand\n$\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=1 - 2 e^{x},$\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\left(1 - 2 e^{x}\\right)-\\left(2 e^{x}\\right)=\\left(1\\right)+\\left(- 2 e^{x}\\right)-\\left(2 e^{x}\\right)=1 - 4 e^{x}\\ne 1.\n$$\n\n- Vector field III gives the required area. Notice that\n$\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=6 y^{2} - \\frac{1}{3}$\nand\n$\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=6 y^{2} + \\frac{2}{3},$\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\left(6 y^{2} + \\frac{2}{3}\\right)-\\left(6 y^{2} - \\frac{1}{3}\\right)=\\left(\\frac{2}{3}\\right)+\\left(6 y^{2}\\right)-\\left(- \\frac{1}{3}\\right)-\\left(6 y^{2}\\right)=1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/hard",
